<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Đurađ I Balšić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1378">1378</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsic-noble-family">Balšić noble family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Đurađ I Balšić was lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, roughly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, from 1362 to 1378, the most capable of the three Balšić brothers who made Zeta independent as the Serbian Empire broke up. He fought rival lords such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-altomanovic">Nikola Altomanović</a> and gained part of his lands after Nikola's defeat in 1373, extending Balšić power along the Adriatic coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durad-i-balsic.jpg" alt="Đurađ I Balšić" width="157" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Balšić noble family · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bal%C5%A1i%C4%87_seal,_January_17,_1368.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Đurađ was the eldest of the three sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-i">Balša I</a>, a petty nobleman who held a single village under the Serbian Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Uroš IV Dušan</a> and was said to be kin to Nemanja. After Dušan's death in 1355 the family began taking Lower Zeta. In 1362 the brothers murdered Đuraš Ilijić, who held Upper Zeta, and were then recognised as provincial lords of Zeta in the charters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1363 Đurađ went to war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thopia-family">Thopia family</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, allied with the Matarangos of the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-thopia">Karl Thopia</a> captured him in a skirmish in the spring of 1364, and he was held until 1366, when the Republic of Ragusa mediated his release; in 1367 he is called the baron of maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. To further their coastal ambitions the brothers converted from Orthodoxy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, formally in 1369.</p><p>Đurađ waged war on Kotor from 1368, hoping to win suzerainty over the town, and besieged it in 1369; Kotor turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, and peace was made by spring 1370, probably through Venetian mediation. Bar paid him an annual tribute of 2,000 ducats, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> complained of armed ships at Bar, Budva and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a> said were Đurađ's. Among the towns he took from the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-altomanovic">Nikola Altomanović</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebinje">Trebinje</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konavle">Konavle</a> and Dračevica, though historians differ on how he acquired them.</p><p>In 1371 he expelled his first wife Olivera, sister of King Marko, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> from Marko, and a year later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pec">Peja</a>. On 30 November 1373 the brothers issued an edict for Ragusa confirming the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a> and recognising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the Serbian Empire. In 1375 Đurađ and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar Hrebeljanović</a> convened the assembly that chose Jefrem as Serbian Patriarch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Đurađ died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Skadar</a>, and rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> passed to his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-ii">Balša II</a>. His neighbours used the moment: the Bosnian Ban Tvrtko I took the rest of his coastal lands by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-kotor">Bay of Kotor</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a> sent forces into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metohija">Metohija</a> and seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a>. His son Konstantin became a local Zetan lord, and descendants of his illegitimate son were among the founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a> in 1444.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Đurađ I Balšić” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His lands were divided between Lazar's Moravian Serbia, Bosnia and <b>Đurađ I Balšić</b> of Zeta.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-nikola-altomanovic">Lordship of Nikola Altomanović</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1356, after Stefan Dušan's death, Balša I and his sons Stracimir, <b>Đurađ I</b> and Balša II took control of Zeta, the land around Lake Skadar and the Gulf of Kotor, ruling from Skadar (Shkodër).</blockquote><a href="/state/zeta-under-the-balsici">Zeta under the Balšići</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.07,19.51&amp;year=1368" aria-label="Shkodër on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5024 666 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Đurađ I Balšić"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5474 936h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5474 936h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.07,19.51&amp;year=1368">What was here in 1368? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
